#!/usr/bin/env ruby
# frozen_string_literal: true

# check-conformance-drift — which cited tests have MOVED since the last sweep.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS (K-1123). `bin/check-conformance-anchors` proves every anchor
# the conformance matrix cites still RESOLVES — the named `it`/`describe`/
# `context` label exists, exactly once, in the file beside it. That is a check on
# the CITATION. It says nothing about the code the citation points at: a cited
# example can be rewritten line by line, keep its label, and go on resolving
# while no longer asserting the statement the matrix claims it covers.
#
# So the matrix is walked instead — by a human, or an agent with a finite budget,
# against a work-list of ~185 rows with no change-detection on it. Predictably
# that samples: the last sweep hand-verified about ten rows, chosen by section
# recency rather than by what had changed, while 257 commits had landed since the
# matrix was rebuilt. Nothing was wrong with the judgement; there was simply no
# way to ask which rows deserved it.
#
# Whether an anchored region still ASSERTS its row is a judgement no script can
# make. Whether it CHANGED is mechanical. This script is the second question and
# only the second question: it fingerprints each anchored region when a sweep
# records a baseline, and afterwards lists the anchors — and, given the matrix,
# the rows — whose region no longer matches. The next sweep re-judges those and
# can say so with a number instead of a sample.
#
# WHAT IS FINGERPRINTED, AND WHY NOT SOMETHING SIMPLER.
#   * NOT the file's blob sha. One line moved in a 500-line spec would flag every
#     anchor in it; `well_known_spec.rb` alone carries 33.
#   * NOT the anchor line alone. That changes only when the label changes, which
#     is precisely the event `check-conformance-anchors` already fails on — the
#     fingerprint would duplicate an existing gate and detect nothing new.
#   * THE ANCHORED REGION: the line the anchor sits on, plus every following line
#     indented MORE deeply than it (blank lines are carried but do not end the
#     region; trailing blanks are trimmed). That is the block the label opens,
#     under both shapes this repo cites — RSpec's `it "…" do … end`, where the
#     body is indented and the closing `end` sits back at the label's column, and
#     `e2e/assistant.sh`'s `assert "…" \` with its indented continuation lines.
#     It is the code the citation offers as evidence, which is the thing whose
#     change should provoke a re-read.
#   * AND NO PADDING FLOOR, which was tried first and MEASURED WRONG (2026-08-27,
#     at the first baseline): 61 of the 537 anchors open nothing and so
#     fingerprint a single line. Extending those to a fixed 3-line window looked
#     like the safe bias until the 61 were actually read:
#     51 are `e2e/assistant.sh` one-line asserts whose line already carries the
#     whole assertion INCLUDING the expected value (the anchor is only the label,
#     so a changed expectation moves the fingerprint), and the file packs 82
#     anchors two lines apart — so the padding would have coupled each assert to
#     its neighbours and flagged three rows for every one that changed. The rule
#     stays "the block the label opens", and where that is one line, one line is
#     what is fingerprinted.
# The digest is the first 16 hex of SHA-256 over the region's exact bytes. This
# detects change; it is not a tamper control and nothing here pretends otherwise.
#
# WHY CONTENT AND NOT `git diff <last-sweep-sha>..HEAD`. Content answers in a
# DIRTY tree, which is where a fix wave lives, and it does not depend on commit
# identity — this repository's history has been rewritten once already, and every
# recorded sha with it. A baseline of bytes survives that; a baseline of shas
# does not.
#
# THE DRIFT REPORT IS NOT A GATE, AND MUST NOT BECOME ONE. Drift here is the
# NORMAL state between sweeps: every legitimate refactor of a cited test produces
# it. A CI job failing on it would have to be silenced immediately, and silencing
# it means re-recording the baseline in the same commit — which sets the interval
# to zero and destroys the only signal the tool has. The baseline must advance
# when a SWEEP has re-judged the rows, never when a commit lands. So the REPORT is
# not a gate, and no workflow runs it.
#
# AND SO THE BARE RUN EXITS 0, WITH DRIFT OR WITHOUT IT (K-1412). It used to exit
# 2 to mean «anchors need re-judging», which was a deliberate distinction from
# exit 1 («cannot answer») and a trap all the same: EVERY OTHER `bin/check-*` in
# this directory exits 0 when it is satisfied, so a session sweeping `bin/check-*`
# in a loop — which is how the gates are run here — read this one member as RED on
# the very state its own output calls «a TRIAGE LIST, not a failure». Two outcomes,
# both bad: a false failure reported to a human, or, worse, a reader who learns to
# ignore a non-zero exit from this directory. A tool whose normal state is non-zero
# teaches people to stop reading exit codes, which is the one signal that cannot be
# faked. The DRIFT COUNT was never the exit code's to carry: it is on the summary
# line, by number, and `--exit-on-drift` is there for the caller who genuinely
# wants a non-zero — the sweep procedure, a future job — asked for explicitly
# rather than inflicted on a directory sweep. Exit 1 still means «cannot answer»,
# in both modes, because that IS a failure: the question was not asked.
#
# `--check` IS A GATE, AND IT ASKS A DIFFERENT QUESTION (the drift-baseline
# staleness gap, 2026-08-27). The baseline is a GENERATED file that had no
# staleness check of any kind, unlike its sibling manifest: a companion gate
# rejects `spec/conformance-anchors.tsv` whenever it is not byte-equal to what
# the exporter would write. MEASURED: the FIRST committed baseline was not
# byte-reproducible from its own committed generator. Its header read «sampling
# ~185 rows blind» where the heredoc below has emitted «sampling the matrix blind»
# since this file's creation commit `918a56f2` — so the committed file had been
# produced by a draft that no longer existed, and nothing in the tree could say
# so. A hand-edited fingerprint, a hand-inserted row and a hand-tweaked header all
# read clean forever for exactly the same reason.
#
# THE MANIFEST'S RULE CANNOT BE COPIED HERE, AND COPYING IT WOULD BE THE WRONG
# FIX. «The baseline must equal what --record would write at head» forces the file
# forward on every commit and sets the interval to zero — the very thing the
# paragraph above rejects. The lag IS the signal. So --check asks about FORM and
# PROVENANCE, and never about head:
#
#   * every row is four tab-separated fields, the fingerprint 16 lowercase hex
#     — which is what `Digest::SHA256.hexdigest(...)[0, 16]` produces — and the
#     ref a short sha or one of the two sentinels, with no duplicated path+anchor
#     pair, no blank line and no comment below the header, because --record emits
#     none of those;
#   * the rows are in `pairs.sort` order, the only order --record can emit;
#   * the header is BYTE-EQUAL to what `baseline_header` renders from the file's
#     OWN recorded stamp, ref, label and count — the same method --record writes
#     it with, so prose drift cannot survive a single run;
#   * every fingerprint RE-MEASURES to its recorded value AT THE COMMIT ITS OWN
#     ROW NAMES, using this file's own `region_at` and `fingerprint`.
#
# That last clause is what catches a hand-edited fingerprint, and it is what makes
# «could THIS generator have written THIS file» a measurement rather than a shape
# test. It pins the baseline to its own recorded PAST, never to head, so a
# baseline three hundred commits behind passes exactly as well as a fresh one.
#
# A PARTIAL RE-JUDGEMENT IS RECORDABLE, AND UNTIL K-1552 IT WAS NOT. The baseline
# used to carry ONE header stamp over ALL of its rows, so a sweep that had
# honestly re-judged part of the triage list had two moves and both were wrong:
# --record everything, which certifies the part nobody read and erases the
# standing list, or record nothing, which hands the next sweep a longer one. It
# only ever grew — MEASURED on this file's own baseline, seven anchors pending on
# 2026-09-06, eight on 09-07, 138 on 09-08, 235 on 09-12, 244 on 09-13. That is
# the FILE FORMAT and not anyone's diligence, and the gate above is what held the
# obvious workaround shut: a hand-mixed file with some rows at head and the rest
# at the header's ref is exactly the file --check exists to refuse.
#
# So provenance moved onto the ROW. Each row carries the ref its fingerprint was
# measured at, as a third field, and --check re-measures each one AT ITS OWN REF.
# `--record --only <scope>` then re-measures the scope, stamps those rows at head,
# and leaves every other row byte-identical — fingerprint AND ref. The file can
# now say «these N were re-judged at this commit, the rest are still pending», and
# the report says which is which. Two sentinels are not commits: `unknown` (git
# could not answer) and `dirty` (the cited file differed from HEAD when the row was
# recorded, so its bytes are in no commit); both are NOTICES and never failures,
# per-row, where the whole file used to be downgraded by one dirty file.
#
# WHAT `--only` MUST NOT BECOME. It is a way to record a re-judgement that
# HAPPENED, never a way to quiet a run: recording a scope nobody read certifies it
# just as falsely as a wholesale --record does, and more quietly, because the
# summary still shows a long pending list. The tool cannot tell the two apart and
# does not pretend to — what it can do is stop forcing an honest partial sweep to
# choose between lying and doing nothing.
#
# ITS LIMITS, because an over-claimed guard is worse than none:
#   (a) A baseline recorded while a cited file was modified in the working tree
#       describes bytes that exist in no commit. --record MEASURES that and stamps
#       it into the `recorded:` line, and --check then reports provenance as
#       UNVERIFIED instead of pretending; commit first if you want a verifiable
#       watermark. It is a NOTICE and not a failure, because recording mid-wave is
#       legitimate and the alternative is a gate that punishes honesty.
#   (b) It is an ACCIDENT control, not a tamper control. Anyone who can hand-edit
#       a row can also run --record. That is equally true of the manifest, whose
#       repair is likewise "re-run the generator".
#   (c) It says NOTHING about whether the baseline is current. That is the drift
#       report's question, and deliberately not a gate.
#
# THE REPAIR FOR A DRIFTED HEADER IS `--reheader`, NOT `--record`, and that matters
# more than it looks. If --record were the only repair, then editing one word of
# the heredoc would force every fingerprint to head and destroy the interval — the
# gate would be a machine for erasing the signal it guards. `--reheader` re-renders
# the header block from the file's own recorded values and touches no fingerprint.
#
# WHAT IT DOES NOT COVER, stated plainly because an over-claimed guard is worse
# than none:
#   * IT SEES BYTES, NOT MEANING. A cited example can keep every byte and stop
#     asserting its row because something it CALLS changed — a shared helper, a
#     `let`, a factory, the implementation under test. Those are invisible here.
#     A clean run is not evidence the matrix is true; it is evidence about where
#     the text moved.
#   * IT SEES THE REGION, NOT THE FILE. Moving a `before` hook, a `let` or a
#     `subject` OUTSIDE the anchored block changes the example's behaviour and
#     not its fingerprint. The sharpest case of this is an anchor that names a
#     COMMENT whose subject is the code BELOW it at the same indentation: the
#     region is then the comment alone and the code it describes is invisible.
#     Measured at the first baseline: three anchors of 537 fingerprint a comment
#     line and nothing else.
#   * IT OVER-REPORTS ON PURPOSE. A re-indentation or a comment tweak inside the
#     region reads as drift. For a triage tool that is the right bias: a false
#     positive costs a glance, a false negative costs a certified stale row.
#   * IT INHERITS THE MANIFEST'S SCOPE. Only anchors exported to
#     `spec/conformance-anchors.tsv` are covered — citations into other repos are
#     not in it and are not seen here.
#
# Usage (stdlib only, no bundle, no database, no network):
#   bin/check-conformance-drift                     # report drift against the baseline
#   bin/check-conformance-drift --matrix PATH       # …naming the matrix rows affected
#   bin/check-conformance-drift --exit-on-drift     # …and exit 2 when regions moved
#   bin/check-conformance-drift --record            # re-record the baseline at the current tree
#   bin/check-conformance-drift --record --only P   # …only the rows whose path matches P
#   bin/check-conformance-drift --record --label T  # …with a free-text label in its header
#   bin/check-conformance-drift --check             # is the baseline one THIS generator wrote?
#   bin/check-conformance-drift --reheader          # re-render its header, touching no fingerprint
#   bin/check-conformance-drift --baseline PATH     # …against a baseline file other than the default
#   bin/check-conformance-drift --self-test         # prove this script is the gate it claims to be
#
# `--only P` is repeatable. A row is in scope when its path equals P, sits under
# it as a directory (`P/…`), or matches it as an `File.fnmatch` glob — so
# `--only kiosk-server/spec` and `--only 'ks*/**/headers_spec.rb'` both work. It
# requires an existing baseline, because a partial re-record is an UPDATE.
#
# Exit codes. The REPORT has two, like every other `bin/check-*` here:
#   0 — it answered. Drift or no drift; the count is on the summary line.
#   1 — the question could not be answered (missing manifest/baseline/file)
# `--exit-on-drift` adds a third for a caller that asks for it: 2 when regions
# moved. `--check` has two, because it IS a gate: 0 the baseline is
# self-consistent, 1 it is not — 2 is never returned. `--record`,
# `--reheader` and `--self-test`: 0 written/green, 1 refused/red, and they say why.

require "digest"
require "English"
require "shellwords"

ROOT     = File.expand_path("..", __dir__)
MANIFEST = File.join(ROOT, "spec", "conformance-anchors.tsv")
DEFAULT_BASELINE = File.join(ROOT, "spec", "conformance-drift-baseline.tsv")

USAGE = <<~TEXT
  usage: bin/check-conformance-drift [--matrix PATH] [--exit-on-drift] [--record [--only P] [--label TEXT]]
         bin/check-conformance-drift --check | --reheader | --self-test
         common:   --baseline PATH  read/write a baseline other than spec/conformance-drift-baseline.tsv
         report:   0 = answered (drift is counted on the summary line, not in the exit code),
                   1 = cannot answer; add --exit-on-drift for 2 = regions moved (triage)
         --check:  0 = the baseline is one this generator could have written, 1 = it is not
TEXT

record        = false
check         = false
reheader      = false
self_test     = false
exit_on_drift = false
matrix        = nil
label         = nil
only          = []
baseline_path = nil

argv = ARGV.dup
until argv.empty?
  arg = argv.shift
  case arg
  when "--record"        then record        = true
  when "--check"         then check         = true
  when "--reheader"      then reheader      = true
  when "--self-test"     then self_test     = true
  when "--exit-on-drift" then exit_on_drift = true
  when "--matrix"        then matrix        = argv.shift
  when "--label"         then label         = argv.shift
  when "--only"          then only         << argv.shift
  when "--baseline"      then baseline_path = argv.shift
  when "-h", "--help"
    puts USAGE
    exit 0
  else
    warn "check-conformance-drift: unknown argument #{arg.inspect}"
    warn USAGE
    exit 1
  end
end

if [record, check, reheader, self_test].count(true) > 1
  warn "check-conformance-drift: --record, --check, --reheader and --self-test are four different " \
       "questions; pass one"
  warn USAGE
  exit 1
end

only = only.compact.reject(&:empty?)
if !only.empty? && !record
  warn "check-conformance-drift: --only scopes a --record; it means nothing on its own"
  warn USAGE
  exit 1
end

BASELINE = baseline_path ? File.expand_path(baseline_path) : DEFAULT_BASELINE

if matrix && !File.file?(matrix)
  warn "check-conformance-drift: --matrix #{matrix}: no such file"
  exit 1
end

# ── the anchored region ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
@bodies = {}

def body_of(rel)
  @bodies.fetch(rel) do
    path = File.join(ROOT, rel)
    @bodies[rel] = File.file?(path) ? File.readlines(path, chomp: true) : nil
  end
end

def indent_of(line)
  line[/\A[ \t]*/].length
end

# The line the anchor sits on, plus every following line indented more deeply
# than it. Blank lines ride along but never end the region, and trailing blanks
# are trimmed by only ever advancing `last` onto a non-blank line.
def region_at(body, idx)
  base = indent_of(body[idx])
  last = idx
  j    = idx + 1
  while j < body.length
    line = body[j]
    unless line.strip.empty?
      break if indent_of(line) <= base

      last = j
    end
    j += 1
  end
  body[idx..last]
end

def fingerprint(lines)
  Digest::SHA256.hexdigest(lines.join("\n"))[0, 16]
end

# `nil` fingerprint = the region could not be taken; the reason rides with it.
def measure(rel, anchor)
  body = body_of(rel)
  return [nil, "file is gone"] if body.nil?

  hits = body.each_index.select { |i| body[i].include?(anchor) }
  case hits.length
  when 1 then nil
  when 0 then return [nil, "anchor no longer occurs in the file"]
  else        return [nil, "anchor is ambiguous (#{hits.length} matches)"]
  end

  region = region_at(body, hits.first)
  [fingerprint(region), "#{region.length} line(s) from :#{hits.first + 1}"]
end

# ── git, read-only ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# stderr is deliberately NOT swallowed: a `fatal:` from git is how you find out
# the question was malformed, and hiding it is how a zero gets manufactured
# (CLAUDE.md, "Stay inside the workspace").
def git(*args)
  out = IO.popen(["git", "-C", ROOT, *args], &:read)
  [out, $CHILD_STATUS.success?]
end

# `git show` hands back the whole blob; File.readlines(chomp: true) does not
# produce a phantom last element for the trailing newline, so neither does this.
def blob_lines(text)
  lines = text.split("\n", -1)
  lines.pop if lines.last == ""
  lines
end

# ── the baseline's FORM: one renderer, one parser, both used by both modes ────
#
# --record WRITES the header with baseline_header and --check RE-RENDERS it with
# the same method from the file's own recorded values. That is the whole
# mechanism: prose that drifted out of one and not the other cannot survive a
# single run, which is precisely what happened to the first baseline and what
# nothing could see.
RECORDED_RE = /
  \A\#\srecorded:\s(?<stamp>\S+)\sat\s(?<head>\S+)
  (?:\s\(working\stree:\s(?<dirty>\d+)\scited\sfile\(s\)\smodified\))?
  (?:\s--\s(?<label>.*))?\z
/x
COUNT_RE       = /\A\# (?<count>\d+) anchors\.\z/
FINGERPRINT_RE = /\A[0-9a-f]{16}\z/
# The per-row provenance field: a git ref, or one of the two sentinels that are
# not commits and say so — `unknown` (git could not answer at record time) and
# `dirty` (the cited file differed from HEAD, so the fingerprinted bytes are in
# no commit). Both are notices in --check, never failures.
REF_RE         = /\A(?:unknown|dirty|[0-9a-f]{7,40})\z/
UNVERIFIABLE   = %w[unknown dirty].freeze

def recorded_line(stamp, head, dirty, label)
  dirty_clause = dirty.to_i.positive? ? " (working tree: #{dirty} cited file(s) modified)" : ""
  "# recorded: #{stamp} at #{head}#{dirty_clause}#{label ? " -- #{label}" : ""}"
end

def baseline_header(count, stamp, head, dirty, label)
  <<~HEAD
    # conformance-drift-baseline.tsv -- GENERATED by bin/check-conformance-drift --record.
    # Do not edit by hand.
    #
    # One `path<TAB>fingerprint<TAB>ref<TAB>anchor` row per anchor in
    # spec/conformance-anchors.tsv. The fingerprint is the first 16 hex of
    # SHA-256 over the ANCHORED REGION -- the line the anchor sits on plus every
    # following line indented more deeply than it, which is the block that label
    # opens. `bin/check-conformance-drift` re-measures and lists what moved, so a
    # conformance sweep can re-judge the rows whose evidence changed instead of
    # sampling the matrix blind.
    #
    # PROVENANCE IS PER ROW, which is what makes a PARTIAL re-judgement
    # recordable (K-1552). `ref` is the commit that row's fingerprint was
    # measured at -- or `dirty` when the cited file differed from HEAD then, or
    # `unknown` when git could not answer. `--record --only <scope>` re-measures
    # the scope, stamps those rows at head and leaves every other row
    # byte-identical, so this file can say «these were re-judged then, the rest
    # are still pending» instead of forcing all-or-nothing. The stamp below is
    # the LAST record operation, not a claim about every row.
    #
    # THIS FILE IS A SWEEP'S WATERMARK, NOT A LOCK. Re-record it when a sweep has
    # RE-JUDGED the drifted rows -- never to make a run quiet. Nothing in CI reads
    # these fingerprints AS DRIFT: drift is the normal state between sweeps and a
    # gate on it would have to be switched off the day it was added. What CI does
    # run is `bin/check-conformance-drift --check`, which asks only whether this
    # file is one its own generator could have written -- row form and order, this
    # header, and that every fingerprint re-measures to its recorded value at the
    # commit ITS OWN ROW names. That never looks at head, so it cannot push the
    # watermark forward. Repair a drifted header with --reheader, which re-renders
    # this block and touches no fingerprint.
    #
    #{recorded_line(stamp, head, dirty, label)}
    # #{count} anchors.
  HEAD
end

# The cited files whose working-tree bytes differ from HEAD. A baseline recorded
# over any of them fingerprints content that is in no commit, so it cannot be
# re-measured later; --record records the COUNT rather than refusing, and --check
# downgrades provenance to a notice when it is non-zero.
def dirty_cited(rels)
  return [] if rels.empty?

  out, ok = git("status", "--porcelain", "--", *rels)
  return [] unless ok

  # `XY path` and `XY old -> new`; the path we care about is the last field, and
  # a quoted path (git core.quotePath) is unquoted enough to compare.
  out.lines.filter_map do |line|
    next if line.strip.empty?

    path = line[3..].to_s.strip.split(" -> ").last.to_s
    path = path[1..-2].to_s if path.start_with?('"') && path.end_with?('"')
    path.empty? ? nil : path
  end.uniq
end

# Is `rel` inside at least one `--only` scope? Equality, a directory prefix, or
# an fnmatch glob — three spellings of the same question, because a scope typed
# by a human is as likely to be a directory as a pattern.
def in_scope?(rel, scopes)
  scopes.any? do |s|
    rel == s || rel.start_with?("#{s}/") || File.fnmatch?(s, rel, File::FNM_PATHNAME)
  end
end

# ── --self-test ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Every arm runs THIS script as a subprocess inside a THROWAWAY GIT REPOSITORY
# built from scratch: a copy of the script under `bin/`, a two-pair manifest, and
# two fixture files with anchored regions. That is the only environment in which
# per-row provenance can be proved, because the claim under test is «this
# fingerprint re-measures at THAT commit» and the arms need two commits they own.
#
# THE FIRST ARM IS K-1552'S MOTIVATING DEFECT ITSELF: a baseline in which SOME
# rows were re-recorded at head and the rest left at an older commit. Under the
# one-stamp format that file was REFUSED — measured then, `--check` exit 1 naming
# the row — which is why a sweep that had honestly judged part of the triage list
# could not write down what it had done. Here it must be accepted, and the arms
# below it prove that accepting it did not cost the accident control: a
# hand-edited fingerprint and a hand-moved ref are each still red.
if self_test
  require "tmpdir"
  require "fileutils"

  SCRIPT = File.expand_path(__FILE__)

  failures = []
  arms     = 0

  def st_run(root, *args)
    out = IO.popen({ "GIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL" => "/dev/null", "GIT_CONFIG_SYSTEM" => "/dev/null" },
                   [File.join(root, "bin", "check-conformance-drift"), *args],
                   err: [:child, :out], &:read)
    [out, $CHILD_STATUS.exitstatus]
  end

  def st_git(root, *args)
    out = IO.popen({ "GIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL" => "/dev/null", "GIT_CONFIG_SYSTEM" => "/dev/null",
                     "GIT_AUTHOR_NAME" => "t", "GIT_AUTHOR_EMAIL" => "t@t",
                     "GIT_COMMITTER_NAME" => "t", "GIT_COMMITTER_EMAIL" => "t@t" },
                   ["git", "-C", root, *args], err: [:child, :out], &:read)
    [out, $CHILD_STATUS.success?]
  end

  def st_fixture(root, marker)
    FileUtils.mkdir_p(File.join(root, "bin"))
    FileUtils.mkdir_p(File.join(root, "spec"))
    FileUtils.cp(SCRIPT, File.join(root, "bin", "check-conformance-drift"))
    File.chmod(0o755, File.join(root, "bin", "check-conformance-drift"))
    File.write(File.join(root, "spec", "conformance-anchors.tsv"), <<~TSV)
      # a fixture manifest
      spec/alpha_spec.rb\tthe ALPHA example
      spec/beta_spec.rb\tthe BETA example
    TSV
    File.write(File.join(root, "spec", "alpha_spec.rb"), <<~RB)
      it "the ALPHA example" do
        expect(1).to eq(1) # #{marker}
      end
    RB
    File.write(File.join(root, "spec", "beta_spec.rb"), <<~RB)
      it "the BETA example" do
        expect(2).to eq(2)
      end
    RB
    st_git(root, "init", "--quiet", "--initial-branch=main")
    st_git(root, "add", "-A")
    st_git(root, "commit", "--quiet", "-m", "fixture")
    st_git(root, "rev-parse", "--short", "HEAD").first.strip
  end

  # Missing rows answer [nil, nil] rather than nil, so an arm that finds a row
  # DELETED reports its own name instead of crashing the run with a NoMethodError
  # — a self-test that dies is a self-test whose output nobody can triage.
  def st_rows(root)
    rows = File.readlines(File.join(root, "spec", "conformance-drift-baseline.tsv"), chomp: true)
               .reject { |l| l.start_with?("#") || l.strip.empty? }
               .to_h { |l| f = l.split("\t", -1); [f[0], [f[1], f[2]]] }
    Hash.new([nil, nil]).merge(rows)
  end

  arm = lambda do |name, ok, detail|
    arms += 1
    failures << "#{name}: #{detail}" unless ok
  end

  Dir.mktmpdir("drift-selftest") do |tmp|
    # ── ARM 1-4: the motivating defect — a MIXED baseline is now recordable ───
    root = File.join(tmp, "mixed")
    c1   = st_fixture(root, "v1")

    out, rc = st_run(root, "--record")
    arm.call("A1 --record writes a baseline", rc.zero?, "exit #{rc}: #{out}")
    rows = st_rows(root)
    arm.call("A2 every row carries its own ref",
               rows.values.map(&:last).uniq == [c1],
               "refs are #{rows.values.map(&:last).inspect}, want all #{c1}")

    # Move ALPHA only, and commit — so the tree has two commits and one drifted row.
    File.write(File.join(root, "spec", "alpha_spec.rb"),
               File.read(File.join(root, "spec", "alpha_spec.rb")).sub("v1", "v2"))
    st_git(root, "commit", "--quiet", "-am", "move alpha")
    c2 = st_git(root, "rev-parse", "--short", "HEAD").first.strip

    out, rc = st_run(root, "--record", "--only", "spec/alpha_spec.rb")
    arm.call("A3 --record --only re-records the scope", rc.zero? && out.include?("PARTIAL"),
               "exit #{rc}: #{out}")
    rows = st_rows(root)
    arm.call("A4 THE MOTIVATING DEFECT: a MIXED baseline is what the file now records",
               rows["spec/alpha_spec.rb"].last == c2 && rows["spec/beta_spec.rb"].last == c1,
               "alpha at #{rows['spec/alpha_spec.rb'].inspect}, beta at #{rows['spec/beta_spec.rb'].inspect}")

    out, rc = st_run(root, "--check")
    arm.call("A5 …and --check ACCEPTS it — this is the exit 1 K-1552 was filed on",
               rc.zero?, "exit #{rc}: #{out}")
    arm.call("A6 --check says how many commits the rows were judged at",
               out.include?("across 2 commit(s)"), out)

    out, _rc = st_run(root, "--record", "--only", "spec/alpha_spec.rb")
    beta_before = st_rows(root)["spec/beta_spec.rb"]
    arm.call("A7 an out-of-scope row is carried through byte-identical",
               beta_before.last == c1, "beta moved to #{beta_before.inspect}")

    # ── ARM 8: the partial record does NOT quiet the pending list ─────────────
    File.write(File.join(root, "spec", "beta_spec.rb"),
               File.read(File.join(root, "spec", "beta_spec.rb")).sub("eq(2)", "eq(3)"))
    out, rc = st_run(root)
    arm.call("A8 the rows nobody judged are STILL reported as pending",
               rc.zero? && out.include?("moved 1") && out.include?("beta_spec.rb"),
               "exit #{rc}: #{out}")
    arm.call("A9 …and the report names the ref each drifted row was last judged at",
               out.include?("last judged at #{c1}"), out)

    # ── ARM 10-11: the accident control still bites, per row ──────────────────
    # Restored by rewriting the bytes, never `git checkout --`: this script is
    # read-only about the repository it walks, and CLAUDE.md's plant-and-restore
    # rule is that a restore comes from a copy of what you changed.
    File.write(File.join(root, "spec", "beta_spec.rb"),
               File.read(File.join(root, "spec", "beta_spec.rb")).sub("eq(3)", "eq(2)"))
    bl  = File.join(root, "spec", "conformance-drift-baseline.tsv")
    raw = File.read(bl)

    File.write(bl, raw.sub(/^spec\/beta_spec\.rb\t([0-9a-f]{16})/) { "spec/beta_spec.rb\t#{'0' * 16}" })
    out, rc = st_run(root, "--check")
    arm.call("A10 a hand-edited fingerprint is still refused", rc == 1 && out.include?("re-measures"),
               "exit #{rc}: #{out}")

    # A row whose REF is moved to the other commit: its bytes do not match there.
    File.write(bl, raw.sub(/^(spec\/alpha_spec\.rb\t[0-9a-f]{16}\t)#{Regexp.escape(c2)}\t/) {
      "#{Regexp.last_match(1)}#{c1}\t"
    })
    out, rc = st_run(root, "--check")
    arm.call("A11 a row stamped at the WRONG commit is refused — provenance is really per row",
               rc == 1 && out.include?("re-measures"), "exit #{rc}: #{out}")
    File.write(bl, raw)

    # ── ARM 12-15: the legacy three-field shape migrates, moving no fingerprint
    legacy = raw.lines.map { |l|
      next l if l.start_with?("#")

      f = l.chomp.split("\t", -1)
      "#{f[0]}\t#{f[1]}\t#{f[3]}\n"
    }.join
    File.write(bl, legacy)
    out, rc = st_run(root, "--check")
    arm.call("A12 a pre-K-1552 three-field baseline is refused, naming --reheader",
               rc == 1 && out.include?("--reheader"), "exit #{rc}: #{out}")

    was_fingerprints = raw.lines.reject { |l| l.start_with?("#") }
                          .to_h { |l| f = l.chomp.split("\t", -1); [f[0], f[1]] }
    out, rc = st_run(root, "--reheader")
    arm.call("A13 --reheader migrates it", rc.zero? && out.include?("three-field"), "exit #{rc}: #{out}")
    migrated = st_rows(root)
    arm.call("A14 …and moves no fingerprint",
               migrated.transform_values(&:first) == was_fingerprints,
               "#{migrated.transform_values(&:first).inspect} != #{was_fingerprints.inspect}")
    out, rc = st_run(root, "--check")
    arm.call("A15 …and the migrated file passes", rc.zero?, "exit #{rc}: #{out}")

    # ── ARM 16-18: --only refuses the shapes that would be a lie ──────────────
    out, rc = st_run(root, "--record", "--only", "spec/nothing_here.rb")
    arm.call("A16 a scope matching nothing is refused, not silently written",
               rc == 1 && out.include?("matches no path"), "exit #{rc}: #{out}")

    out, rc = st_run(root, "--only", "spec/alpha_spec.rb")
    arm.call("A17 --only without --record is refused", rc == 1 && out.include?("means nothing on its own"),
               "exit #{rc}: #{out}")

    root2 = File.join(tmp, "nobaseline")
    st_fixture(root2, "v1")
    out, rc = st_run(root2, "--record", "--only", "spec/alpha_spec.rb")
    arm.call("A18 a partial record with no baseline to update is refused",
               rc == 1 && out.include?("nothing here to update"), "exit #{rc}: #{out}")

    # ── ARM 19: a dirty cited file is stamped `dirty`, per row, not per file ──
    root3 = File.join(tmp, "dirty")
    st_fixture(root3, "v1")
    File.write(File.join(root3, "spec", "alpha_spec.rb"),
               File.read(File.join(root3, "spec", "alpha_spec.rb")).sub("v1", "uncommitted"))
    out, rc = st_run(root3, "--record")
    arm.call("A19 --record stamps only the MODIFIED file's row `dirty`",
               rc.zero? && st_rows(root3)["spec/alpha_spec.rb"].last == "dirty" &&
                 st_rows(root3)["spec/beta_spec.rb"].last != "dirty",
               "exit #{rc}: #{st_rows(root3).inspect}")
    out, rc = st_run(root3, "--check")
    arm.call("A20 …and --check verifies the clean row while NOTICING the dirty one",
               rc.zero? && out.include?("NOTICE") && out.include?("1 of 2"), "exit #{rc}: #{out}")
  end

  if failures.empty?
    puts "check-conformance-drift --self-test: OK — #{arms} arm(s) green."
    puts "  A4/A5 are K-1552's motivating defect: a baseline mixing re-judged rows with pending ones."
    puts "  A10/A11 are the price of accepting it — a hand-edited fingerprint and a hand-moved ref stay red."
    exit 0
  end

  warn "check-conformance-drift --self-test: FAIL — #{failures.length} of #{arms} arm(s) red."
  failures.each { |f| warn "  x #{f}" }
  exit 1
end

# ── --check / --reheader ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
if check || reheader
  unless File.file?(BASELINE)
    warn "check-conformance-drift: no baseline at #{BASELINE} — there is nothing to check."
    warn "Record one with `bin/check-conformance-drift --record`."
    exit 1
  end

  raw   = File.read(BASELINE)
  lines = raw.split("\n", -1)
  lines.pop if lines.last == ""

  # `parse` defects make the file unreadable as a baseline, so --reheader refuses
  # on them; `form` and `prov` are what --check adds on top.
  parse       = []
  form        = []
  prov        = []
  notes       = []
  legacy_rows = []

  parse << "the file does not end in a newline; --record always writes one" unless raw.end_with?("\n")

  header = lines.take_while { |l| l.start_with?("#") }
  body   = lines.drop(header.length)

  stamp = head = label = count = nil
  dirty = 0

  if header.length < 2
    parse << "the header is #{header.length} line(s). --record writes a comment block ending in " \
             "`# recorded: <date> at <ref>` and `# N anchors.`"
  else
    if (m = RECORDED_RE.match(header[-2]))
      stamp = m[:stamp]
      head  = m[:head]
      label = m[:label]
      dirty = m[:dirty].to_i
    else
      parse << "the second-to-last header line is not a recorded-stamp: #{header[-2].inspect}"
    end

    if (c = COUNT_RE.match(header[-1]))
      count = c[:count].to_i
    else
      parse << "the last header line is not a `# N anchors.` count: #{header[-1].inspect}"
    end
  end

  rows = []
  seen = {}
  body.each_with_index do |line, i|
    n = header.length + i + 1

    if line.strip.empty?
      parse << "line #{n}: blank — --record emits no blank lines, and the reader SKIPS this, so it is invisible content"
      next
    end
    if line.start_with?("#")
      parse << "line #{n}: a comment below the header — --record emits none, and the reader SKIPS it: #{line.inspect}"
      next
    end

    fields = line.split("\t", -1)
    # A THREE-field row is the pre-K-1552 shape, and it is readable: its
    # provenance is the header's ref, which is what every row shared then. It is
    # not a parse defect (--reheader migrates it, materialising that ref per row
    # and touching no fingerprint) but --check refuses it below, because a file
    # left in the old shape cannot record a partial re-judgement.
    legacy = fields.length == 3
    unless [3, 4].include?(fields.length)
      parse << "line #{n}: #{fields.length} tab-separated field(s), want 4 (path, fingerprint, ref, anchor)"
      next
    end

    if legacy
      rel, fp, anchor = fields
      ref = nil
    else
      rel, fp, ref, anchor = fields
    end
    if rel.empty? || anchor.empty?
      parse << "line #{n}: empty #{rel.empty? ? 'path' : 'anchor'}"
      next
    end
    unless FINGERPRINT_RE.match?(fp)
      parse << "line #{n}: #{fp.inspect} is not a fingerprint — `Digest::SHA256.hexdigest(...)[0, 16]` " \
               "is 16 lowercase hex digits and nothing else"
      next
    end
    if ref && !REF_RE.match?(ref)
      parse << "line #{n}: #{ref.inspect} is not a provenance ref — --record writes a short commit sha, " \
               "or `dirty`/`unknown` when the bytes cannot be pinned to one"
      next
    end
    if (first = seen[[rel, anchor]])
      parse << "line #{n}: duplicates line #{first} — --record writes one row per manifest pair"
      next
    end

    legacy_rows << n if legacy
    seen[[rel, anchor]] = n
    rows << [rel, fp, ref, anchor]
  end

  keys = rows.map { |rel, _fp, _ref, anchor| [rel, anchor] }
  if keys != keys.sort
    i = (1...keys.length).find { |k| (keys[k - 1] <=> keys[k]) == 1 }
    parse << "the rows are not in `pairs.sort` order, which is the only order --record emits: " \
             "#{keys[i].inspect} follows #{keys[i - 1].inspect}"
  end

  if count && count != rows.length
    parse << "the header says #{count} anchors and #{rows.length} row(s) follow. --record writes the " \
             "MANIFEST's count in the header and one row per MEASURABLE anchor, and it exits 1 when " \
             "those differ — a baseline in this state was recorded over a red bin/check-conformance-anchors"
  end

  # ── --reheader: re-render the header, keep every fingerprint ───────────────
  if reheader
    unless parse.empty?
      warn "check-conformance-drift --reheader: refusing — the baseline cannot be read back as one."
      parse.each { |p| warn "  x #{p}" }
      warn "--reheader only re-renders the header; it cannot repair the rows. Fix them, or re-record."
      exit 1
    end

    was = header.join("\n") + "\n"
    now = baseline_header(count, stamp, head, dirty, label)
    # A legacy row's provenance IS the header's ref — that is what the one-stamp
    # format meant — so materialising it per row states what the file already
    # said, and no fingerprint moves. `dirty` overrides it: the header recorded
    # that some cited file was modified, and nothing in the old format says
    # WHICH, so the honest per-row answer for all of them is «not pinnable».
    migrated = dirty.positive? ? "dirty" : (head || "unknown")
    File.write(BASELINE,
               now + rows.map { |rel, fp, ref, anchor|
                 "#{rel}\t#{fp}\t#{ref || migrated}\t#{anchor}\n"
               }.join)
    if legacy_rows.empty? && was == now
      puts "check-conformance-drift --reheader: header was already what this generator emits; " \
           "#{rows.length} row(s) rewritten byte-for-byte."
    else
      if legacy_rows.any?
        puts "check-conformance-drift --reheader: #{legacy_rows.length} row(s) were the pre-K-1552 " \
             "three-field shape; each now carries #{migrated.inspect}, which is the provenance the " \
             "header already claimed for them. No fingerprint moved."
      end
      puts "check-conformance-drift --reheader: header re-rendered (#{was.lines.length} -> " \
           "#{now.lines.length} line(s)); #{rows.length} fingerprint(s) untouched."
    end
    exit 0
  end

  unless legacy_rows.empty?
    form << "#{legacy_rows.length} row(s) carry the pre-K-1552 three-field shape (first at line " \
            "#{legacy_rows.first}). Provenance is per ROW now — a one-stamp file cannot record a " \
            "partial re-judgement, which is the whole of K-1552. Repair with " \
            "`bin/check-conformance-drift --reheader`: it materialises the header's own ref into " \
            "every row and moves no fingerprint."
  end

  # ── the header, byte-for-byte against this generator ───────────────────────
  if stamp && count
    want = baseline_header(count, stamp, head, dirty, label)
    got  = header.join("\n") + "\n"
    if want != got
      wl = want.split("\n", -1)
      gl = got.split("\n", -1)
      i  = (0...[wl.length, gl.length].max).find { |k| wl[k] != gl[k] }
      form << "the header is not what this generator emits (first difference at header line #{i + 1}):\n" \
              "      file:      #{gl[i].inspect}\n" \
              "      generator: #{wl[i].inspect}\n" \
              "      Repair with `bin/check-conformance-drift --reheader`, which rewrites the header " \
              "and no fingerprint. Do NOT --record: that would drag every fingerprint to head."
    end
  end

  # ── provenance: every fingerprint re-measures AT THE COMMIT ITS ROW NAMES ──
  #
  # Per ROW since K-1552, which is the mechanism a partial re-record needs: rows
  # stamped at three different commits are three verifiable groups, not one
  # unverifiable file. The two sentinels are counted and never asserted, so one
  # row recorded over a modified file no longer disarms the whole check.
  verified_rows = 0
  if head.nil?
    notes << "provenance not checked: the recorded stamp did not parse, so there is no ref to measure at."
  elsif rows.empty? || legacy_rows.length == rows.length
    notes << "provenance not checked: no row carries a per-row ref to measure at."
  else
    at_ref      = {}
    mismatched  = []
    unmeasured  = []
    unverifiable = Hash.new(0)
    absent_refs = {}

    rows.each do |rel, fp, ref, anchor|
      ref ||= "unknown"
      if UNVERIFIABLE.include?(ref)
        unverifiable[ref] += 1
        next
      end

      if absent_refs.fetch(ref) { absent_refs[ref] = !git("rev-parse", "--verify", "--quiet", "#{ref}^{commit}").last }
        unmeasured << "#{rel}  #{anchor.inspect}: #{ref} is not a commit in this repository"
        next
      end

      lines_at = at_ref.fetch([ref, rel]) do
        text, ok = git("show", "#{ref}:#{rel}")
        at_ref[[ref, rel]] = ok ? blob_lines(text) : nil
      end

      if lines_at.nil?
        unmeasured << "#{rel}  #{anchor.inspect}: no such path at #{ref}"
        next
      end

      hits = lines_at.each_index.select { |i| lines_at[i].include?(anchor) }
      unless hits.length == 1
        unmeasured << "#{rel}  #{anchor.inspect}: " \
                      "#{hits.empty? ? "anchor absent at #{ref}" : "anchor ambiguous at #{ref} (#{hits.length} matches)"}"
        next
      end

      got = fingerprint(region_at(lines_at, hits.first))
      if got == fp
        verified_rows += 1
      else
        mismatched << "#{rel}  #{anchor.inspect}: recorded #{fp}, re-measures #{got} at #{ref}"
      end
    end

    unverifiable.each do |sentinel, n|
      notes << "#{n} row(s) name #{sentinel.inspect} rather than a commit, so their fingerprints cannot be " \
               "re-measured. " +
               (sentinel == "dirty" ?
                 "They were recorded over a working tree that differed from HEAD; re-record those paths " \
                 "from a clean tree if you want them verifiable." :
                 "They were recorded where git could not answer; re-record inside a git checkout.")
    end

    unless mismatched.empty?
      prov << "#{mismatched.length} of #{rows.length} fingerprint(s) do NOT re-measure to their recorded " \
              "value at the commit their own row names. This generator cannot have produced them from " \
              "that tree:\n" +
              mismatched.first(12).map { |m| "      #{m}" }.join("\n") +
              (mismatched.length > 12 ? "\n      … and #{mismatched.length - 12} more" : "")
    end
    unless unmeasured.empty?
      prov << "#{unmeasured.length} of #{rows.length} row(s) name an anchor that does not resolve uniquely " \
              "at their own recorded ref — the baseline describes a tree in which its own rows were not " \
              "measurable:\n" +
              unmeasured.first(12).map { |m| "      #{m}" }.join("\n") +
              (unmeasured.length > 12 ? "\n      … and #{unmeasured.length - 12} more" : "")
    end
  end

  problems = parse + form + prov

  unless notes.empty?
    puts "check-conformance-drift --check: NOTICE"
    notes.each { |n| puts "  - #{n}" }
    puts
  end

  unless problems.empty?
    warn "check-conformance-drift --check: FAIL — #{problems.length} defect(s) in spec/#{File.basename(BASELINE)}."
    problems.each { |p| warn "  x #{p}" }
    warn ""
    warn "This file is GENERATED and nothing else in the tree can tell you it stopped being so."
    warn "The repairs are: --reheader for the header, --record for the rows (but --record advances"
    warn "the watermark to head, which is only earned once a sweep has re-judged the drifted rows)."
    exit 1
  end

  puts "check-conformance-drift --check: OK — #{File.basename(BASELINE)} is one this generator could have written."
  puts "  #{rows.length} row(s), last recorded #{stamp} at #{head}#{label ? " -- #{label}" : ""}"
  puts "  rows: 4 fields each, 16-hex fingerprints, no duplicates, in `pairs.sort` order, header count agrees"
  puts "  header: byte-identical to `baseline_header` re-rendered from this file's own values"
  if verified_rows.positive?
    by_ref = rows.map { |_rel, _fp, ref, _anchor| ref }.tally.reject { |r, _| UNVERIFIABLE.include?(r) }
    puts "  provenance: #{verified_rows} of #{rows.length} fingerprint(s) re-measure at the commit their " \
         "OWN row names, across #{by_ref.size} commit(s)"
    by_ref.sort_by { |ref, n| [-n, ref] }.first(6).each { |ref, n| puts "    #{ref}  #{n} row(s)" }
    puts "    … and #{by_ref.size - 6} more commit(s)" if by_ref.size > 6
  end
  puts "(This says the baseline is SELF-CONSISTENT. It deliberately says nothing about whether it is"
  puts "CURRENT — the lag is the drift signal, and a check that demanded head would destroy it.)"
  exit 0
end

unless File.file?(MANIFEST)
  warn "check-conformance-drift: missing #{MANIFEST} — it is generated with the matrix"
  exit 1
end

# ── the manifest: the same path/anchor pairs bin/check-conformance-anchors gates
pairs = File.readlines(MANIFEST, chomp: true).filter_map do |line|
  next if line.start_with?("#") || line.strip.empty?

  rel, anchor = line.split("\t", 2)
  next if rel.nil? || anchor.nil? || anchor.empty?

  [rel, anchor]
end

if pairs.empty?
  warn "check-conformance-drift: #{MANIFEST} lists no anchors — nothing to fingerprint"
  exit 1
end

# ── --record ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
if record
  head = `git -C #{Shellwords.escape(ROOT)} rev-parse --short HEAD 2>/dev/null`.strip
  head = "unknown" if head.empty?
  stamp = Time.now.strftime("%Y-%m-%d")

  # ── the scope, and what is PRESERVED outside it ────────────────────────────
  #
  # Wholesale --record rewrites every row at head. `--only` re-measures the
  # scope and carries every other baseline row through byte-identical —
  # fingerprint AND ref — which is the whole point: a sweep that judged part of
  # the list records that part and leaves the rest visibly pending.
  preserved = []
  if only.any?
    unless File.file?(BASELINE)
      warn "check-conformance-drift --record --only: no baseline at #{BASELINE}."
      warn "A partial re-record is an UPDATE; there is nothing here to update. Record a whole one first."
      exit 1
    end

    bad = []
    File.readlines(BASELINE, chomp: true).each_with_index do |line, i|
      next if line.start_with?("#") || line.strip.empty?

      fields = line.split("\t", -1)
      unless fields.length == 4
        bad << "line #{i + 1}: #{fields.length} field(s), want 4"
        next
      end
      rel, fp, ref, anchor = fields
      next if in_scope?(rel, only)

      preserved << [rel, fp, ref, anchor]
    end

    unless bad.empty?
      warn "check-conformance-drift --record --only: refusing — #{bad.length} row(s) are not the " \
           "four-field shape this can carry through untouched:"
      bad.first(6).each { |b| warn "  x #{b}" }
      warn "Run `bin/check-conformance-drift --reheader` first; it migrates them and moves no fingerprint."
      exit 1
    end
  end

  scoped = only.any? ? pairs.select { |rel, _anchor| in_scope?(rel, only) } : pairs
  if only.any? && scoped.empty?
    warn "check-conformance-drift --record --only: #{only.map(&:inspect).join(', ')} matches no path in " \
         "spec/#{File.basename(MANIFEST)} — a scope that selects nothing would rewrite the header and " \
         "re-judge nothing."
    exit 1
  end

  dirty_paths = head == "unknown" ? [] : dirty_cited(scoped.map(&:first).uniq.sort)
  dirty = dirty_paths.length

  measured = []
  missing  = []
  scoped.each do |rel, anchor|
    fp, why = measure(rel, anchor)
    if fp.nil?
      missing << "#{rel}: #{why} -- #{anchor.inspect}"
      next
    end
    ref = head == "unknown" ? "unknown" : (dirty_paths.include?(rel) ? "dirty" : head)
    measured << [rel, fp, ref, anchor]
  end

  rows = (preserved + measured).sort_by { |rel, _fp, _ref, anchor| [rel, anchor] }
  # The header's count is what this operation INTENDED to have a row for —
  # preserved rows plus the scope's manifest pairs — so a count that disagrees
  # with the rows below is the signature of an unmeasurable anchor, which is
  # what the exit 1 at the bottom is about.
  out = +baseline_header(preserved.length + scoped.length, stamp, head, dirty, label)
  rows.each { |rel, fp, ref, anchor| out << "#{rel}\t#{fp}\t#{ref}\t#{anchor}\n" }

  File.write(BASELINE, out)
  if only.any?
    puts "wrote #{File.basename(BASELINE)} — PARTIAL: #{measured.length} of #{scoped.length} scoped " \
         "anchor(s) re-recorded at #{head} (#{stamp}), #{preserved.length} row(s) left untouched at " \
         "their own refs."
    puts "  scope: #{only.map(&:inspect).join(', ')}"
    puts "  The untouched rows are STILL PENDING re-judging and the report will keep saying so."
  else
    puts "wrote #{File.basename(BASELINE)} (#{measured.length} of #{scoped.length} anchors, #{stamp} at #{head})"
  end

  if dirty.positive?
    warn ""
    warn "check-conformance-drift: NOTICE — #{dirty} cited file(s) differ from #{head} in the working tree,"
    warn "so those rows are stamped `dirty` rather than with a commit and `--check` cannot verify them."
    warn "That is recorded per row rather than refused; commit the cited files and re-record if you"
    warn "want a watermark whose provenance is checkable."
  end

  unless missing.empty?
    warn ""
    warn "check-conformance-drift: #{missing.length} anchor(s) could not be measured and are NOT in the baseline."
    warn "bin/check-conformance-anchors is the gate for this; it should be red too."
    missing.each { |m| warn "  x #{m}" }
    exit 1
  end
  exit 0
end

# ── the report ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
unless File.file?(BASELINE)
  warn "check-conformance-drift: no baseline at #{BASELINE}"
  warn "Record one with `bin/check-conformance-drift --record`; until then there is"
  warn "nothing to compare against and this cannot answer the question."
  exit 1
end

baseline     = {}
baseline_ref = {}
recorded     = nil
File.readlines(BASELINE, chomp: true).each do |line|
  recorded ||= line[/\A#\s*recorded:\s*(.+)\z/, 1]
  next if line.start_with?("#") || line.strip.empty?

  fields = line.split("\t", -1)
  # Four fields since K-1552 (path, fingerprint, ref, anchor); a three-field row
  # is the pre-K-1552 shape and still READS — the report is not the gate for it,
  # `--check` is, and reading it lets a sweep triage an un-migrated file.
  if fields.length >= 4
    rel, fp, ref, anchor = fields[0], fields[1], fields[2], fields[3..].join("\t")
  else
    rel, fp, anchor = fields
    ref = nil
  end
  next if rel.nil? || fp.nil? || anchor.nil? || anchor.empty?

  baseline[[rel, anchor]] = fp
  baseline_ref[[rel, anchor]] = ref
end

if baseline.empty?
  warn "check-conformance-drift: #{BASELINE} holds no fingerprints"
  exit 1
end

# ── optional: name the matrix rows, so the output is a work-list ─────────────
#
# The manifest carries no row ids -- deliberately: it is published in this repo
# and the matrix is not. Given the matrix, its rows are mapped onto the anchors
# here. The resolver mirrors the exporter's (`ks/` abbreviates kiosk-server's
# spec dir, a bare `e2e` means e2e/assistant.sh); citations into other repos are
# not in the manifest and are skipped.
PATH_TOKEN = %r{(?:ks/|e2e/|kiosk-[a-z0-9-]+/|kiosk\.tech/)[A-Za-z0-9_][A-Za-z0-9_./-]*\.(?:rb|sh|erb|rake|md|json|yml|yaml|html)}
ANCHOR_RE  = %r{@"((?:[^"\\]|\\.)*)"}
CITATION   = %r{(?<path>#{PATH_TOKEN}|(?<![A-Za-z0-9_/.-])e2e)(?<anchors>(?:#{ANCHOR_RE})+)}

rows_for = Hash.new { |h, k| h[k] = [] }
if matrix
  File.readlines(matrix, chomp: true).each do |line|
    row = line[/\A- (SPEC-\d+[a-z]?)/, 1]
    next if row.nil?

    line.scan(CITATION) do
      m = Regexp.last_match
      path = m[:path]
      next if path.start_with?("kiosk.tech/")

      rel = path == "e2e" ? "e2e/assistant.sh" : path.sub(%r{\Aks/}, "kiosk-server/spec/kiosk/server/")
      m[:anchors].scan(ANCHOR_RE) do
        anchor = Regexp.last_match(1).gsub('\\"', '"').gsub("\\\\", "\\")
        list = rows_for[[rel, anchor]]
        list << row unless list.include?(row)
      end
    end
  end
end

moved   = []
fresh   = []
broken  = []
same    = 0

pairs.sort.each do |rel, anchor|
  was = baseline[[rel, anchor]]
  fp, why = measure(rel, anchor)

  if fp.nil?
    broken << [rel, anchor, why]
  elsif was.nil?
    fresh << [rel, anchor, fp, why]
  elsif was == fp
    same += 1
  else
    moved << [rel, anchor, was, fp, why]
  end
end

retired = baseline.keys.reject { |k| pairs.include?(k) }

def label_for(rows_for, rel, anchor)
  rows = rows_for[[rel, anchor]]
  rows.empty? ? "" : "#{rows.join(', ')}  "
end

def report(title, entries, rows_for)
  return if entries.empty?

  puts
  puts title
  entries.group_by { |e| e[0] }.each do |rel, group|
    puts "  #{rel}"
    group.each do |entry|
      _rel, anchor, *rest = entry
      puts "    #{label_for(rows_for, rel, anchor)}#{anchor.inspect}"
      puts "      #{rest.compact.join('  ')}"
    end
  end
end

total = pairs.length
puts "check-conformance-drift: #{total} anchors, baseline last recorded #{recorded || '(unstamped)'}"
puts "  unchanged #{same}   moved #{moved.length}   new #{fresh.length}   unmeasurable #{broken.length}" \
     "#{retired.empty? ? '' : "   retired #{retired.length}"}"
puts "  matrix rows named from #{matrix}" if matrix
puts "  (no --matrix given: anchors only, no SPEC row ids)" unless matrix

# WHEN EACH UNCHANGED ROW WAS LAST JUDGED. The header stamp is the last --record
# operation and says nothing about a row a partial re-record left alone, so the
# per-row refs are the only place «these N were re-judged then, the rest are
# still pending» is written down (K-1552).
judged = pairs.sort.filter_map { |rel, anchor| baseline_ref[[rel, anchor]] }.tally
unless judged.empty?
  legacy_n = judged.delete(nil).to_i
  puts "  judged at: " + (judged.sort_by { |ref, n| [-n, ref] }.first(6)
                                .map { |ref, n| "#{ref} #{n}" }.join("   ") +
                          (judged.size > 6 ? "   … #{judged.size - 6} more commit(s)" : "") +
                          (legacy_n.positive? ? "   (#{legacy_n} row(s) carry no per-row ref: run --reheader)" : ""))
end

report("MOVED — the anchored region changed since the baseline; re-judge these rows:",
       moved.map { |rel, anchor, was, now, why|
         [rel, anchor, "#{was} -> #{now}", "last judged at #{baseline_ref[[rel, anchor]] || '(no ref)'}", why]
       }, rows_for)
report("NEW — cited since the baseline was recorded, never judged against it:",
       fresh.map { |rel, anchor, fp, why| [rel, anchor, fp, why] }, rows_for)
report("UNMEASURABLE — bin/check-conformance-anchors is the gate for these:",
       broken.map { |rel, anchor, why| [rel, anchor, why, nil] }, rows_for)

unless retired.empty?
  puts
  puts "RETIRED — in the baseline, no longer cited by the matrix (informational):"
  retired.sort.each { |rel, anchor| puts "  #{rel}  #{anchor.inspect}" }
end

if moved.empty? && fresh.empty? && broken.empty?
  puts
  puts "No anchored region has moved. (This says where the text is unchanged. It does NOT"
  puts "say the tests still assert their rows — see the header for what this cannot see.)"
  exit 0
end

puts
puts "#{moved.length + fresh.length + broken.length} anchor(s) need re-judging. This is a TRIAGE LIST, not a failure:"
puts "walk them, then re-record the baseline with --record so the next sweep starts from here."
puts "Judged only PART of it? `--record --only <path-or-glob>` stamps that scope at head and leaves"
puts "every other row at its own ref, so the rest keeps showing up here instead of being certified."

# K-1412: exit 0, because this is a report and not a gate — the count above is
# where drift is reported. `--exit-on-drift` is the opt-in for a caller that
# wants a non-zero to branch on; a `bin/check-*` sweep does not, and used to read
# the 2 as red on a tool whose normal state this is.
exit 2 if exit_on_drift

puts "(exit 0: this is a report. Pass --exit-on-drift if you need a non-zero status to branch on.)"
exit 0
